<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.4.3: Factoring the GCF from Polynomials</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01640</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.4.3: Factoring the GCF from Polynomials</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4931f0d7-dfcd-4977-8f73-f37cbb2b51b0</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>To factor a GCF from a polynomial and represent it in factored form, we must first find the GCF.</para>
            <para>Let’s look at an example: \(7z^5 − 21sz^3\).</para>
            <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Find the GCF of the terms in the polynomial:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_07_06_01.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>The GCF of the terms is \(7z^3\).</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Apply the Distributive Property “in reverse” to factor out the GCF from each term.</para>
            <para>\(7z^5 − 21sz^3 = {\style{color:red}7}
    {\style{color:red}z}
    ^
    {\style{color:red}3} \cdot z^2 − {\style{color:red}7}
    {\style{color:red}z}
    ^
    {\style{color:red}3} \cdot 3s\)</para>
            <para>\(\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;= 7z^3(z^2 − 3s)\)</para>
            <para>The factored form is \(7z^3(z^2 − 3s)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <para>Work individually on the following problems.</para>
      <para>Factor the GCF from each polynomial. Remember that if the leading coefficient is negative, then the GCF will be negative.</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(5x^3 − 25x^2\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(5x^2(x − 5)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(8x^3y − 10x^2y^2 + 12xy^3\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(2xy(4x^2 − 5xy + 6y^2)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(−5y^3 + 35y^2 − 15y\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(−5y(y^2 − 7y + 3)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(15x^3y − 3x^2y^2 + 6xy^3\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(3xy(5x^2 − xy + 2y^2)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(−4p^3q − 12p^2q^2 + 16pq^2\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(−4pq(p^2 + 3pq − 4q)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(5x(x + 1) + 3(x + 1)\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\((x + 1)(5x + 3)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(3b(b − 2) − 13(b − 2)\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\((b − 2)(3b − 13)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(6m(m − 5) − 7(m − 5)\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\((m − 5)(6m − 7)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
    <note class="self-check">
      (    <para>Factor: \(8a^3b + 2a^2b^2 − 6ab^3\).</para>)
        </note>
        <section>
          <title>Additional Resources</title>
          
    <section>
      <title>Factoring the GCF from Polynomials</title>
      <para>It is sometimes useful to represent a number as a product of factors, for example, 12 as \(2 \cdot 6\) or \(3 \cdot
  4\). In algebra, it can also be useful to represent a polynomial in factored form. We will start with a product, such
  as \(3x^2+15x\), and end with its factors, \(3x(x + 5)\). To do this, we apply the Distributive Property “in
  reverse.” We state the Distributive Property here just as you saw it in earlier chapters and “in
  reverse.”</para>
      <note>
        <para>DISTRIBUTIVE PROPERTY</para>
        
        <para>If \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) are real numbers, then</para>
        <para>\(\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;a(b + c) = ab + ac\) and \(ab + ac = a(b + c)\)</para>
        <para>The form on the left is used to multiply. The form on the right is used to factor.</para>
      </note>
      <para>So how do you use the Distributive Property to factor a polynomial? You just find the GCF of all the terms and write
the polynomial as a product!</para>
      <para>Example 1</para>
      <para>Factor: \(8m^3-12m^2n+20mn^2\).</para>
      <para>How to factor the greatest common factor from a polynomial:</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Find the GCF of all the terms of the polynomial. For this problem, find the GCF of \(8m^3\), \(12m^2n\), and
  \(20mn^2\).</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_07_08_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Rewrite each term as a product using the GCF. In this case, rewrite \(8m^3\), \(12m^2n\), and \(20mn^2\) as products
  of their GCF, \(4m\).</para>
      <list>
        <item>\(8m^3 = {\style{color:red}4}
    {\style{color:red}m} \cdot 2m^2\)</item>
        <item>\(12m^2n = {\style{color:red}4}
    {\style{color:red}m} \cdot 3mn\)</item>
        <item>\(20mn^2 = {\style{color:red}4}
    {\style{color:red}m} \cdot 5n^2\)</item>
      </list>
      <para>\(8m^3-12m^2n+20mn^2\)
  \({\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red}m} \cdot 2m^2 - {\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red}m} \cdot 3mn + {\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red}m} \cdot 5n^2\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Use the “reverse” Distributive Property to factor the expression.</para>
      <para>\(4m(2m^2-3mn+5n^2)\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Check by multiplying the factors.</para>
      <para>\(4m(2m^2-3mn+5n^2)\)
  \(4m \cdot 2m^2 - 4m \cdot 3mn + 4m \cdot 5n^2\)
  \(8m^3-12m^2n+20mn^2\)</para>
      <para>When the leading coefficient is negative, we factor the negative out as part of the GCF.</para>
      <para>Example 2</para>
      <para>Factor: \(−4a^3 + 36a^2 − 8a\).</para>
      <para>The leading coefficient is negative, so the GCF will be negative.</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Rewrite each term using the GCF, \(−4a\).</para>
      <para>\(
  {\style{color:red}-}
  {\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red}a} \cdot a^2 −
  {\style{color:red}(}
  {\style{color:red}-}
  {\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red}a}
  {\style{color:red})} \cdot 9a +
  {\style{color:red}(}{\style{color:red}-}
  {\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red}a}
  {\style{color:red})} \cdot 2\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Factor the GCF.</para>
      <para>\(−4a(a^2 − 9a + 2)\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Check.</para>
      <para>\(−4a(a^2 − 9a + 2)\)
  \(−4a \cdot a^2 − (−4a) \cdot 9a + (−4a) \cdot 2\)
  \(−4a^3 + 36a^2 − 8a\)</para>
      <para>So far, our greatest common factors have been monomials. In the next example, the greatest common factor is a
  binomial.</para>
      <para>Example 3</para>
      <para>Factor: \(3y
  {\style{color:red}(}
  {\style{color:red}y} \;{\style{color:red}+}\;     {\style{color:red}7}
  {\style{color:red})}
  − 4{\style{color:red}(}
  {\style{color:red}y}\;     {\style{color:red}+} \;{\style{color:red}7}
  {\style{color:red})}\).</para>
      <para>The GCF is the binomial \(y + 7\).</para>
      <para>\(3y(y + 7) − 4(y + 7)\).</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Factor the GCF, \((y + 7)\).</para>
      <para>\((y + 7)(3y − 4)\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Check on your own by multiplying.</para>
      <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Factoring the GCF from Polynomials</title>
      <para>Factor.</para>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>\(9xy^2 + 6x^2y^2 + 21y^3\)</para>
            <para>\(−4b^3 + 16b^2 − 8b\)</para>
            <para>\(4m(m + 3) − 7(m + 3)\)</para>
            <para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Here is how to factor the GCF from polynomials.</para>
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
              <item>\(3y^2(3x + 2x^2 + 7y)\)</item>
            </list>
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
              <item>\(−4b(b^2 − 4b + 2)\)</item>
            </list>
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
              <item>\((m + 3)(4m − 7)\)</item>
            </list>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
        </section>
        

  </content>
